From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: shell32: a move to the Recycle Bin from the profile is a rename

Deleting a 15 GB video from Videos said 100% at once, sat there for a
minute, and Cancel said "Cancelling..." and then finished the delete
anyway. A user's profile (C:\users\<name>) is a bind mount of their home,
and the Trash (~/.local/share/Trash) is in the home through another mount
(/home): rename() between two mounts fails with EXDEV even on one disk, so
MoveFileW fell back to copying the whole file. The progress window counted
the item before its work started, and the copy took no progress routine,
so Cancel could not stop it. On Windows the Recycle Bin is on the file's
own volume and a move to it is a rename.

trash_file looks for the same Trash folder through the file's own mount:
an ancestor of the file holding .local\share\Trash where the .trashinfo
just made is seen too; the move there is a rename (MoveFileEx without
COPY_ALLOWED). Restore does the same the other way. When no such folder
is there (another disk), the move still copies, now with the progress
window's routine: the bar follows the bytes and Cancel stops the copy,
leaves the file where it was, and ends the delete (no "delete it for
good?" after a Cancel). A delete counts an item when it is gone, so 100%
means done. The Recycle Bin's InvokeCommand also takes its verbs by name
("undelete", "delete"); it took only command offsets and said S_OK.

Gate: test-trashmount (user and mount namespace: the profile bind-mounted
from the home; same inode in the Trash and back on Restore; a file on
tmpfs still reaches the Trash whole). Mutant SG_MUTANT_TRASH_OTHER_MOUNT.

